{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothBaseCase","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothBaseCase","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothIndex","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothHeightBound"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":358,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L358-L400","doc":"**The `m = 1` case of Roth's lemma.**\n\n    `|lead P| ≤ q^e`,  `q ≥ 2`,  `gcd(p,q) = 1`,  `P ≠ 0`,  `d ≠ 0`\n      ⟹  `index_d(P; p/q) ≤ e/d`.\n\nWith Roth's hypothesis `H(P) ≤ q^{εd}` — so `e = εd` — this reads `index ≤ ε`, which is what\nthe induction on the number of variables consumes.\n\nThe proof is by contradiction on the *realised* index.  Because `index` is an infimum over a\nfinite support, and in one variable every support element is `Finsupp.single () n`, the index\nis literally `n₀/d` for some natural `n₀`.  So `e/d < index` forces `e < n₀`, hence\n`(e+1)/d ≤ index`, hence `q^{e+1} ≤ |lead P| ≤ q^e` — impossible for `q ≥ 2`.\n\n⚠ That integrality step is why `q ≥ 2` rather than `q > 0`: with `q = 1` the powers do not\nseparate and the argument gives nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_pow_abs_leadingCoeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_pow_abs_leadingCoeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":320,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L320-L356","doc":"**The base case with a *power* bound, and no rounding anywhere.**\n\n`|lead|^Q ≤ q^S` gives `index ≤ S/(Q·d)` directly.  ⚠ This is the shape `RothGammaFull`'s\nclause 4 produces (`b^{Mq} ≤ K₁^{s₁p}`), and taking it directly is the *only* route that keeps\nthe recursion sound: going through `index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le` requires an `e : ℕ` with\n`S ≤ Q·e`, whose rounding costs `1/d` — and `RothLemmaStatement.two_sqrt_expands` shows any\npositive perturbation is amplified to `≈ 4` by the recursion, however small.\n\nThe proof is the same contradiction as the unpowered version, raised to the `Q`-th power:\n`q^{n₀Q} = (q^{n₀})^Q ≤ |lead|^Q ≤ q^S` against `S < n₀Q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_pow_abs_coeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_pow_abs_coeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":277,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L277-L318","doc":"**§10's estimate with a *power* bound on both sides: `a^Q ≤ H^S ⟹ index ≤ S/(Q·d)`.**\n\nThe shape `Γ₁`'s clause (i) actually delivers.  §11 states it as `b ≤ K₁^{s₁t}` with `t` a\nrational `0 < t ≤ 1`; writing `t = p/q` and clearing the exponent gives `b^q ≤ K₁^{s₁p}`, an\ninequality between integers with no root and no ceiling in it.  Going through\n`index_le_of_abs_coeff_le` instead would need an `e : ℕ` with `s₁p ≤ q·e`, and that rounding\ncosts `1/d` — which `RothLemmaStatement.two_sqrt_expands` shows the recursion amplifies to `≈ 4`\nhowever small it starts.\n\nSame contradiction as the unpowered form, raised to the `Q`-th power:\n`H^{n₀Q} = (H^{n₀})^Q ≤ (max |trailing| |leading|)^Q ≤ a^Q ≤ H^S` against `S < n₀Q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_abs_coeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_abs_coeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L238-L275","doc":"**§10's estimate, log-free: `a ≤ H^e ⟹ index ≤ e/d`.**\n\n`H = max(|p|, |q|)` and `a` bounds every coefficient of `P` — which is exactly\n`InRothPolySet`'s third clause at one variable.  Taking logarithms turns this into\n`index ≤ log a / (d log H)`, which is §10 as Mahler states it; the exponential form is kept\nbecause the recursion is sensitive to rounding (`RothLemmaStatement.two_sqrt_expands`) and a\nlogarithm would introduce it.\n\nThe proof is the same integrality contradiction as `index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le`, run on\n`H` instead of `q`: the index *is* `n₀/d`, so `e < n₀` would force `H^{e+1} ≤ H^{n₀} ≤ a ≤ H^e`.\n\n⚠ `2 ≤ H` is not a convenience.  At `H = 1` the point is `0`, `±1`, or `∞`, the bound\n`log a / (d log H)` is infinite, and the estimate says nothing — the same reason\n`index_le_of_abs_leadingCoeff_le` needs `q ≥ 2`, one level up. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div_and_height_pow_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div_and_height_pow_le","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L195-L209","doc":"**The one-variable index is `n₀/d`, and `H^{n₀}` divides into the two extreme\ncoefficients** — §10's `H^j ≤ |A|`, restated against `index`.\n\n`H = max(|p|, |q|)` is the height of the point.  This is the statement the module's previous\n⚠-block said was missing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div_and_pow_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div_and_pow_le","kind":"theorem","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L182-L193","doc":"**The one-variable index is `n₀/d`, and `q^{n₀}` divides into the leading coefficient.**\n\nThe `Q^j` half of §10, restated against `index`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.taylor_coeff_eq_zero_of_index_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.taylor_coeff_eq_zero_of_index_eq","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L167-L180","doc":"**Every Taylor coefficient below the realised index vanishes.**\n\nThe bridge both height halves consume: `exists_index_eq_div` says the index *is* `n₀/d`, so\n`taylor_coeff_eq_zero_of_le_index` applies at `k = n₀` with `le_of_eq`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_index_eq_div","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L126-L165","doc":"**The one-variable index is literally `n₀/d` for a natural `n₀`.**\n\n`translate` is invertible, so a non-zero polynomial has a non-zero translate and the support is\nnon-empty; an infimum over a finite non-empty set is attained; and in one variable every\nsupport element is `Finsupp.single () n`.\n\n⚠ No hypothesis on `d`: `idxWeight` reads `n/d` in `ℚ`, where `n/0 = 0`, so the identity\nholds at `d = 0` too.  The consumers need `d ≠ 0`; this does not.\n\nExtracted because **three** consumers need it — the `q^j` half, the `H^j` half, and the\npowered form — and it had been written out twice verbatim. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_le_index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_le_index","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L111-L122","doc":"The height bound of `RothHeightBound.lean`, restated against `index`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.taylor_coeff_eq_zero_of_le_index","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.taylor_coeff_eq_zero_of_le_index","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L86-L109","doc":"A lower bound `k/d` on the index kills every Taylor coefficient below `k` — the hypothesis\n`pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero` asks for.\n\nIn one variable every multi-index is `Finsupp.single () n`, so `idxWeight` is just `n/d`, and\n`index ≥ k/d` says exactly that no `n < k` occurs in the support of the translate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.pUnitAlgEquiv_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.pUnitAlgEquiv_translate","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L72-L82","doc":"**`translate` is `Polynomial.taylor`** under the one-variable dictionary.  Both are\n\"substitute `X + α` for `X`\", so this is really a naturality statement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.coeff_pUnitAlgEquiv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.coeff_pUnitAlgEquiv","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothBaseCase.lean#L53-L70","doc":"Coefficients correspond along `n ↦ Finsupp.single () n`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}